Glimblewick, a gnome warlock, trotted through Stranglethorn Vale with his succubus, Velvet. She’d just seduced a panther so he could blast it with shadow bolts. “See? Faster than a voidwalker,” he chirped. Then a rogue ambushed him. Velvet’s Lash of Pain tickled the rogue, but he shrugged it off and stunned Glimblewick. The succubus had no taunt, no shield—just a kiss that reduced threat. The rogue ignored her and gutted the gnome. As Glimblewick’s soulstone popped him back, he realized: a voidwalker would have held aggro and sacrificed itself. Velvet pouted. “You’re the one who forgot to dismiss me before pulling.”

The answer: Most warlocks use voidwalkers because they provide reliable tanking and a damage-absorbing shield, which is safer for solo questing in a world with frequent ganks and unpredictable pulls. The succubus’s damage and crowd control are stronger on paper, but require more micro-management and offer no survivability against multiple or aggressive enemies. [end of text]


